Here is a breakdown of every pod running in your cluster, grouped by the Kubernetes architecture component it belongs to.

1. Control Plane Components
These pods form the "brain" of your Kubernetes cluster. They run as static pods on your control-plane node (mindstix-assignment-cluster-control-plane).

kube-apiserver-...

Component: API Server (kube-apiserver)

Purpose: The central front door of Kubernetes. Every command you run (like kubectl), along with communication between cluster components, goes directly through this API.

etcd-...

Component: etcd Database

Purpose: The cluster's key-value database. It stores the state and configuration data of the entire cluster (e.g., deployments, secrets, namespaces).

kube-scheduler-...

Component: Kubernetes Scheduler (kube-scheduler)

Purpose: Assigns unscheduled pods to nodes based on resource availability, constraints, and affinity rules.

kube-controller-manager-...

Component: Controller Manager (kube-controller-manager)

Purpose: Runs background control loops (e.g., Node Controller, ReplicaSet Controller) to ensure the current state of the cluster matches your desired state.

2. Networking & Cluster Add-ons
These pods run in the kube-system namespace to handle internal network routing, DNS resolution, and Pod-to-Pod communication across your 3-node cluster.

coredns-... (2 replicas)

Component: CoreDNS

Purpose: Provides internal DNS resolution within the cluster so pods can find and talk to services by name (e.g., my-service.default.svc.cluster.local).

kindnet-... (3 pods: flq8g, h9v2g, x2vpz)

Component: CNI Plugin (Kindnet)

Purpose: The Container Network Interface (CNI) specifically used by Kind. It sets up IP addresses for pods and routes traffic across nodes. There is 1 pod running on each of your 3 nodes (managed via a DaemonSet).

kube-proxy-... (3 pods: 8vbhk, pp2qp, t2nt6)

Component: Network Proxy (kube-proxy)

Purpose: Manages network rules on each node using iptables/ipvs. It enables network communication to your Pods from inside or outside the cluster (Services abstraction). Runs on all 3 nodes via a DaemonSet.

